# CONSULT-63 — storm MECHANISM analysis (Opus, mechanism-reasoning angle)

Grounded in the vendored 9.3b source (D:\Code\milesss-v9.3b\win\sdk\src\sdk).
Key files read: mssdig.cpp (SS_serve / SS_fill dispatch / DS mix-ahead accounting),
wavefile.cpp:11902 (SS_fill — the actual mix+ASI-decode pass), mssstrm.cpp (open/prime/async IO).

## The load-bearing architectural fact I found (drives the whole ranking)

The mixer pass is `SS_serve` (MSSTimer thread, ~AIL_MM_PERIOD). In the DirectSound path it:
  1. briefly locks the Miles mutex ONLY around GoodGetCurrentPosition and the DS Lock/Unlock;
  2. computes how many fragments are already filled ahead of the play cursor (`frags_filled`)
     and a target `DS_frag_mix_ahead` (in-game = 16ms of fragments);
  3. for each fragment still needing fill, calls `win_fill` → `SS_fill` **with the mutex NOT
     held**. SS_fill (wavefile.cpp:11902) is where the real cost lives:
       - it starts any *scheduled* samples (calls AIL_API_start_sample inline, 12013),
       - it walks EVERY active ASI sample and calls `ASI_stream_process(...)` to
         prefetch/decode a block (12026-12065) — MP3 decode happens HERE, per pass, per sample,
       - then mixes all busses into the fragment.

Corollary that decides everything below: **app-side AIL_* calls cost ~0ms because they only
allocate handles / set state / schedule; the compressed→PCM MP3 decode is deferred onto the
mixer thread's fill pass.** So a decode-cost spike is INVISIBLE to every app-side probe, to the
main-thread watchdog, and to the 0ms open/close measurements. That is exactly the shape of this
storm.

A second load-bearing fact: an MP3 stream that was just opened+primed has only its **compressed**
input buffer filled by async IO (mssstrm.cpp: prime loads 7/8 of `bufsize` of *compressed* bytes;
`primeleft` is a compressed-byte counter). Its **PCM output reservoir is empty at start**. The
first ~N mixer passes after AIL_start_stream must therefore do EXTRA MP3 decode work to build the
reservoir lead from zero up to steady-state depth. That build-up window is the "~1 second."

---

## Candidate mechanisms

### M1 — Mixer-thread ASI-decode overrun during the new-stream decode ramp, tipped over by the concurrent one-shot decode  ★ TOP
- Resource under contention: **CPU time inside a single SS_fill pass vs. the 16ms in-game
  mix-ahead budget.**
- State: freshly-opened stream has an EMPTY PCM output reservoir. For ~1s the mixer must decode
  more MP3 per pass than at steady state to build the reservoir lead. During that ramp the
  per-pass headroom above 16ms is at its thinnest. Adding a one-shot MP3 at that instant forces
  the same pass to ALSO init a new ASI decoder (12013 start + first ASI_stream_process, 12045)
  and decode its frames — additive load that pushes total pass time past the fragment duration.
- Why CRACKLE not silence/latency: when a fill pass overruns, the play cursor advances into
  fragments that were not refilled in time → the DS ring replays stale/partial PCM → periodic
  underrun clicks (crackle), not a clean gap. As soon as the new stream's reservoir reaches
  steady depth (~1s) the per-pass cost drops back under budget and it self-heals.
- Consistency vs. every locked fact:
  - 0ms open/close: ✓ decode is on the mixer thread, not the app call. STRONGLY explains it.
  - Watchdog silent / no main stall: ✓ cost is off-main-thread.
  - Idle transition clean (a/b): ✓ stream ramp ALONE fits the budget; no added one-shot decode.
  - Already-looping harvester hum clean: ✓ its ASI reservoir is already at steady depth — no
    ramp, negligible marginal cost.
  - Door 8s later clean: ✓ by then the stream reservoir is steady, so the one-shot's marginal
    decode fits under budget. This is the single fact that most cleanly discriminates M1 from
    everything mutex/teardown-related.
  - Prompt release no help: ✓ cost is on OPEN+prime+first-decode, not teardown.
  - No volume steps: ✓ underrun, not gain.
  - Strain: assumes the marginal one-shot decode is enough to cross the threshold on a modern CPU
    at only ~16ms budget. Plausible precisely because in-game mix-ahead is a mere 16ms (thin) and
    MP3 frame decode + ASI init is bursty. Note the eliminated "192ms premix" was the LOAD-phase
    budget (64→192ms), NOT the in-game 16ms — so the in-game budget lever is UNTESTED and M1 is
    not contradicted by that elimination.

### M2 — DS false-dropout re-prime discontinuity (mixer-thread scheduling jitter mistaken for a real underrun)
- Resource/state: SS_serve's dropout detector (mssdig.cpp:1851): if `timer-DS_last_timer >
  DS_skip_time` OR the fill fragment passed the play cursor, it forces `frags_filled=0` and the
  foreground path re-primes the whole queue from `dig->first` (1597-1619 / SS_foreground_service
  1597). A re-prime rewrites fragments the hardware is about to play → an audible splice.
- Why CRACKLE: the re-prime overwrites/duplicates queued PCM at a non-zero-crossing → a burst of
  clicks each time it trips.
- Consistency: explains 0ms/off-main/no-volume-step the same way as M1. Explains the transition
  specificity only if the transition makes SS_fill passes long enough (or preempts the timer long
  enough) to trip the detector. STRAIN: `DS_skip_time = DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_CNT * DIG_DS_FRAGMENT_SIZE`
  ≈ the full ring (~256ms) — a single long pass won't trip it; you'd need the boosted MSSTimer
  thread to be starved >256ms, which the boosted priority argues against, and the watchdog seeing
  no main stall weakly argues against a whole-machine CPU cliff. More likely a SECONDARY amplifier
  of M1 (once M1 makes a pass overrun, the "fill passed play cursor" clause at 1851 can co-fire)
  than the primary. Ranked #2 because it is the same-family alternative and shares an instrument.

### M3 — First-decode burst of the primed compressed buffer serialized with the one-shot's ASI init
- Essentially the mechanism *inside* M1 stated as its own hypothesis: the very first
  ASI_stream_process on the new stream (12045) is disproportionately expensive (parses MP3 header,
  fills granule reservoir from the 7/8-primed compressed block in one go) and, if it lands in the
  same pass as the one-shot's first ASI_stream_process, the two bursts serialize on the single
  mixer thread. Same consistency profile as M1; I fold it into M1 rather than rank separately —
  it's the "why the FIRST ~passes specifically" refinement, not a distinct resource.

### M4 — Miles global-mutex contention between the app start-batch and SS_serve's position/Lock
- Resource: the Miles mutex. App holds it across the start batch; SS_serve needs it at
  mssdig.cpp:1788 (GetCurrentPosition) and 1915/1947 (DS Lock/Unlock). If the app holds it while
  the play cursor advances, the timer thread stalls and can fall behind → underrun.
- Why CRACKLE: delayed refill → underrun clicks.
- Consistency: superficially fits off-main/0ms. **But directly contradicted by a locked fact:**
  cycling the lock per-sound (which REDUCES hold time and thus this contention) made audio WORSE,
  not better. If mutex hold were the mechanism, shortening it would have helped. REFUTED as primary
  by the evidence pack. Keep only as a note.

### M5 — Stream-slot instant-reuse: ASI pipeline teardown/re-init of the reused slot races the new decode
- Resource: the per-slot ASI decoder pipeline (SP_ASI_DECODER stage) and its OUTPUT_RESERVOIR.
  Old stream closes, new opens ~16ms later into the SAME slot; the ASI stage is torn down and
  re-created. If a one-shot ASI is allocated in the same window they may momentarily share
  provider scratch or the reservoir is read while being re-inited.
- Why CRACKLE: glitched decode output (garbage/partial PCM) rather than underrun.
- Consistency: fits off-main/0ms. STRAIN: MP3 ASI instances are per-sample (separate stream
  objects), so true shared-scratch corruption is unlikely; and if it were corruption you'd expect
  it independent of the *8s-later* control (the slot is reused there too on later transitions).
  The clean-at-8s control argues the problem is reservoir-DEPTH/timing (M1), not slot re-init
  corruption. Lower confidence.

### M6 — milesasync IO-thread saturation delays the new stream's compressed refill, starving decode
- Resource: the background milesasync IO thread + app TreeFile callbacks. At the transition the
  new stream primes (7/8) AND the door one-shot does a whole-file load through the SAME TreeFile
  layer. If IO is momentarily backed up, the new stream's compressed refill lags → the decoder
  runs dry → underrun.
- Why CRACKLE: compressed starvation → decoder produces short/empty PCM → underrun clicks.
- Consistency: fits off-main/0ms. STRAIN: the pack already ELIMINATED "live compressed-refill
  starvation" (the STARVED edges were a slot-reuse logging artifact; Miles reuses slots
  instantly). So the direct starvation form is disproven; the only residual is IO-thread *CPU*
  contention with decode, which overlaps M1. Low confidence, largely eliminated.

---

## Ranking (confidence)

1. **M1 — mixer-thread ASI-decode overrun of the 16ms in-game mix-ahead during the new-stream
   reservoir ramp, tipped by the concurrent one-shot decode.**  ~60%. Uniquely explains the
   8s-later clean control, the idle-clean control, the 0ms/off-main invisibility, and the ~1s
   duration (= reservoir build time) with no strain.
2. **M2 — DS false-dropout/real-timer-starvation re-prime discontinuity.**  ~20%. Same instrument
   as M1; likely a co-firing amplifier rather than root, but must be ruled in/out because it
   changes the fix (widen dropout tolerance / raise timer robustness vs. widen decode budget).
3. M4 mutex contention ~6% (refuted-as-primary by the per-sound-lock regression).
4. M5 slot re-init corruption ~7%. 5. M6 IO/refill ~4% (mostly eliminated). Residual ~3%.

---

## Decisive experiments

### For #1 (M1) — two independent convictions, engine-side first

**(A) Engine-side ablation (no DLL build needed).** Two-variable decode-load test on the crisp
repro (cantina door as zone theme ends):
  - Replace the DOOR one-shot asset with an uncompressed PCM/ADPCM WAV (kills its ASI-decode
    path) while keeping the music as MP3 streams. If the storm VANISHES → the one-shot's marginal
    ASI decode is the tipping load ⇒ M1 convicted.
  - Independently, keep the MP3 door but convert the MUSIC playlist to a PCM/ADPCM stream (kills
    the stream decode ramp). If clean → the stream reservoir ramp is the thin-headroom component
    ⇒ M1 convicted from the other side.
  Either result convicts M1; both clean = decisive. (Both being MP3 is the only storm state.)

**(B) The untested budget lever.** Raise the IN-GAME DS mix-ahead only — set
`DIG_DS_MIX_FRAGMENT_CNT` (via AIL_set_preference) so the in-game cushion goes 16ms → ~64-96ms,
and re-run the exact repro. This is NOT the eliminated "load premix 192ms" (that was the
load-phase budget). If the storm attenuates/disappears with a larger in-game cushion, underrun-vs-
budget (M1) is confirmed; if totally unchanged, M1 is weakened toward M2/M5.

**Instrumented mss32.dll (escalation) — exact instrumentation to convict/refute M1:**
File `win/sdk/src/sdk/wavefile.cpp`, function `SS_fill` (line 11902). `start_time = rrGetTime()`
is already captured at 11908, and per-stage costs `dig->asi_times[0..3]`, `dig->mix_time`, and
`dig->n_active_samples` are already accumulated. Add at the END of SS_fill a lightweight ring-log
(OutputDebugString or a lock-free ring the engine can drain) of:
  `{ pass_wall_us = rrGetTime()-start_time, sum(asi_times), mix_time, n_active_samples,
     n_ASI_samples_processed, fragment_budget_us }`.
CONVICT M1 if, during the storm window ONLY, `pass_wall_us` (and specifically `sum(asi_times)`)
spikes ABOVE the fragment budget and `n_ASI_samples_processed` jumps by exactly the one-shot(s)
added — and returns under budget as the window ends ~1s later. REFUTE M1 if pass_wall stays well
under budget throughout the storm (then the crackle is not a fill-time overrun → pivot to M2/M5).

### For #2 (M2) — decisive experiment

**Instrumented mss32.dll**, file `win/sdk/src/sdk/mssdig.cpp`, function `SS_serve`:
  - at line 1851 (the dropout branch) log a counter + the `(timer - dig->DS_last_timer)` delta and
    which clause fired (`skip_time` exceeded vs. `DS_last_frag < cur_frag`);
  - at the re-prime block (1597-1619 / SS_foreground_service prime path) log each re-prime event;
  - also log the inter-pass MSSTimer delta (timeGetTime at SS_serve entry) to catch true
    timer-thread starvation.
CONVICT M2 if the dropout/re-prime counters fire repeatedly *during the storm window* (and the
inter-pass delta blows past `DS_skip_time`). REFUTE M2 (and thereby point back to M1) if those
counters are silent while M1's pass_wall spikes — i.e., the passes overrun but never trip the
dropout logic, meaning the crackle is pure per-pass underrun, not a re-prime splice.

Note: because both instruments live in one rebuilt mss32.dll and both fire on the same MSSTimer
thread, ONE instrumented-DLL session (with the SS_fill ring-log + SS_serve dropout counters)
adjudicates M1 vs. M2 simultaneously — that is the single highest-value escalation.
